## Summary

| File | Change | Why |
|------|--------|-----|
| `src/store/chats.js` | New `mergeValue` / `mergeMessage` / `mergeMessages` / `applyChatMessages`; `mergeChat` now patches `messages` instead of replacing it; `addMessage`, `removeMessage`, `updateMessages` write via `applyChatMessages` | A reload no longer swaps the conversation array or creates new message objects. A streamed chunk mutates `message.content` only, so only the entry rendering that field re-renders |
| `src/components/chat/Chat.vue` | `messages` computed returns the previous array while the items are the same; `sameFiles` generalised to `sameList` | `filter()` always returned a new array, giving `ChatMessageList` a new prop identity → forced update of its unstable slots (composer, `ChatIntelliSense`, modals) on every refresh |

### Notes / behaviour changes to be aware of

- `ChatEntry` has `watch: { message() { this.loadThreadChat() } }`. It used to fire on *every* reload (because message objects were replaced), triggering a thread reload per streamed chunk. With `ChatEntry` props now keeping their identity it no longer fires for content updates — `loadThreadChat()` still runs in `created()`, and thread content updates through the store as usual. If you want it to persist explicitly, change it to `'message.doc_id'`.
- `mergeValue` merges plain objects in place, so a field that the server *omits* on a later read keeps its previous value (same as the existing chat-level `mergeChat` behaviour). Add explicit `null`s server-side if a field must be cleared.

